"access_score": -3}]}, {"id": "20260627193303904454_2fdd9012", "type": 
"Evidence", "text": "“This paper presents an analysis of the iconography 
surrounding the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates. By revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, 
the paper aims to contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's 
significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical 
scholarship and recent discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions 
related to the ring's motifs and their implications.”", "source_ids": 
["20260627193302935626_02aac6ec"], "all_source_ids": 
["20260627193302935626_02aac6ec"], "citations": [2], "source_profiles": 
[{"source_id": "20260627193302935626_02aac6ec", "title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON 
THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_R
ING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": "paper", "quality_score": 7, "access_score": 
0}]}, {"id": "20260627193303953830_a174b9bc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Key 
takeaway: “The text explores the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor' through 
iconographic analysis. Contradictions in technical analysis highlight the 
importance of iconography in Aegean studies. The connection between incurved 
altars and the ring's depiction supports its authenticity. The ring's 
iconography may reflect cultural practices from the 1920s. Generations of 
scholars have debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings.”", 
"source_ids": ["20260627193302935626_02aac6ec"], "all_source_ids": 
["20260627193302935626_02aac6ec"], "citations": [2], "source_profiles": 
[{"source_id": "20260627193302935626_02aac6ec", "title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON 
THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_R
ING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": "paper", "quality_score": 7, "access_score": 
0}]}, {"id": "20260627193303995544_1198c3ed", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“The 
Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring first described by the archaeologist Arthur
Evans in 1925. According to Evans, it was made on Minoan Crete in the Late 
Bronze Age and discovered in a Mycenaean tholos tomb near the ancient site of 
Pylos in Messenia, in southwest Greece. Evans considered it to show scenes from 
the underworld, and to illustrate connections between Minoan belief and ancient 
Egyptian religion.”", "source_ids": ["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], 
"all_source_ids": ["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], "citations": [3], 
"source_profiles": [{"source_id": "20260627193302956439_f6f077a9", "title": 
"Ring of Nestor - Wikipedia", "url": 
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_of_Nestor", "source_type": "other", 
"quality_score": -1, "access_score": 0}]}, {"id": 
"20260627193304034312_497c4098", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Infobox data: 
“Material: Gold; Size: Bezel: 3.29 cm × 2.18 cm; Hoop inner diameter: 1.4 to 
1.56 cm; Hoop band: 0.82 cm × 0.35 cm; Weight: 31.76 g; Discovered: Before 1907 
Kakovatos, Messenia, Greece (reported); Present location: Ashmolean Museum; 
Period: Late Minoan I (c. 1700–c. 1450 BCE) (claimed); 20th century CE 
(claimed); Culture: Minoan (disputed).”", "source_ids": 
["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], "all_source_ids": 
["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], "citations": [3], "source_profiles": 
[{"source_id": "20260627193302956439_f6f077a9", "title": "Ring of Nestor - 
Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_of_Nestor", 
"source_type": "other", "quality_score": -1, "access_score": 0}]}, {"id": 
"20260627193304073748_ee2a7913", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Since its 
discovery, the ring's authenticity has been debated, with several scholars 
considering it a modern fake, and others defending it as a genuinely ancient 
artefact. Arguments in both directions have been made on the grounds of 
iconographical consistency with other Minoan artworks, and based upon the 
potential role of Émile Gilliéron fils, an art restorer, suspected forger, and 
prolific collaborator of Evans's, in the ring's discovery. Other arguments for 
authenticity include the wear evident upon the ring and microscopic analysis of 
its engraving technique, while circumstantial evidence against it has been 
adduced from Evans's convoluted story of its origins and the fortuitous 
connections between its iconography and Evans's own ideas about the links 
between Minoan and Egyptian religion.”", "source_ids": 
["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], "all_source_ids": 
["20260627193302956439_f6f077a9"], "citations": [3], "source_profiles": 
[{"source_id": "20260627193302956439_f6f077a9", "title": "Ring of Nestor - 
Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_of_Nestor", 
"source_type": "other", "quality_score": -1, "access_score": 0}]}, {"id": 
"20260627193304114224_debc5d8d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“The first subject
of the present communication is a truly royal treasure of gold rings and of 
bead-seals in the same metal found in a Mycenaean rock tomb near the site of 
Thisbê in Boeotia. The find was made in 1915, at a time when war conditions 
diverted the course of discovery from official channels, and a fortunate chain 
of circumstances now enables me not only to describe but to exhibit to the 
Society the whole hoard. Under the circumstances it is impossible without a 
breach of confidence to give all the details, but, from what I have been able to
ascertain, the discovery was made by a peasant in a chamber-tomb excavated in 
the rock, by the village of Dombrena. Other objects that I myself was able 
either to see or secure, found in the same group of tombs, but not authenticated
as coming from the chamber containing the treasure, were bronze spear-heads and 
a short sword with the flange running round the extremity of the hilt, belonging
to a very late Mycenaean type.”", "source_ids": 
["20260627193302975889_96514190"], "all_source_ids": 
["20260627193302975889_96514190"], "citations": [4], "source_profiles": 
[{"source_id": "20260627193302975889_96514190", "title": "‘The Ring of Nestor’: 
A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A Sepulchral Treasure of Gold 
Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia", "url": 
"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-hellenic-studies/article/rin
g-of-nestor-a-glimpse-into-the-minoan-afterworld-and-a-sepulchral-treasure-of-go
ld-signetrings-and-beadseals-from-thisbe-boeotia/5E465BD1031E9251C962E138498E6CF
A", "source_type": "paper", "quality_score": 7, "access_score": 0}]}, {"id": 
